1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,521
A deployment plan can send its package straight to another Orchard Core site,

2
00:00:05,521 --> 00:00:07,373
which imports it on arrival.

3
00:00:07,373 --> 00:00:10,368
The Remote Deployment module makes that possible.

4
00:00:10,368 --> 00:00:14,780
In this video, you connect two sites and send a plan from one to the other.

5
00:00:19,380 --> 00:00:21,737
Remote deployment involves two sites.

6
00:00:21,737 --> 00:00:26,451
The source site holds the deployment plan, and the destination site receives the

7
00:00:26,451 --> 00:00:28,041
package and imports it.

8
00:00:28,041 --> 00:00:32,920
You create a remote client on the destination, and a remote instance on the source.

9
00:00:33,670 --> 00:00:37,966
When you execute a plan, the source builds a package and posts it

10
00:00:37,966 --> 00:00:41,597
to the destination, with a client name and an Api Key.

11
00:00:41,597 --> 00:00:47,830
If both match its remote client, the destination imports the package at once, without asking you.

12
00:00:48,580 --> 00:00:51,120
Before you start, check three things.

13
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:56,319
Deployment and Remote Deployment are enabled on both sites, under Tools, Features.

14
00:00:56,319 --> 00:01:00,691
The source can reach the destination, over HTTPS on a real site, and

15
00:01:00,691 --> 00:01:04,590
the destination has the features that the plan's steps need.

16
00:01:08,847 --> 00:01:12,928
Let's start on the destination, a site called Staging Site.

17
00:01:12,928 --> 00:01:16,381
Under Tools, Deployments, we open Remote Clients.

18
00:01:16,381 --> 00:01:20,147
The list is empty, because this site trusts no source.

19
00:01:20,627 --> 00:01:26,376
A remote client is the destination's record of a source site that may send it packages.

20
00:01:26,376 --> 00:01:28,477
We select Add Remote Client.

21
00:01:28,947 --> 00:01:31,693
The Client Name identifies the source site.

22
00:01:31,693 --> 00:01:33,094
We enter Main Site.

23
00:01:33,094 --> 00:01:36,847
The source must send this name exactly as it is written here.

24
00:01:37,303 --> 00:01:42,166
The Api Key is the shared secret, and the field hides what you type.

25
00:01:42,166 --> 00:01:47,883
Use a long, random value, and keep a copy, because the source needs the same key.

26
00:01:48,346 --> 00:01:49,638
We select Create.

27
00:01:49,638 --> 00:01:54,242
A notification confirms the remote client, and the list shows its name, with

28
00:01:54,242 --> 00:01:55,926
Edit and Delete buttons.

29
00:02:00,126 --> 00:02:05,372
The source needs the address of the destination's import endpoint, which has three parts.

30
00:02:05,372 --> 00:02:09,623
They are the address of the destination site, the prefix of its tenant,

31
00:02:09,623 --> 00:02:12,826
if the tenant has one, and the path of the endpoint.

32
00:02:14,526 --> 00:02:19,686
Back on the source site, called Orchard Core, we open Tools, Deployments, Remote

33
00:02:19,686 --> 00:02:22,566
Instances, and select Add Remote Instance.

34
00:02:28,523 --> 00:02:32,000
A remote instance is the source's record of a destination.

35
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,803
Its Name appears in the list of targets when you execute a plan,

36
00:02:35,803 --> 00:02:37,813
so choose one people recognize.

37
00:02:37,813 --> 00:02:39,063
We enter Staging.

38
00:02:39,594 --> 00:02:44,374
For the Url, we enter the endpoint, built the way the slide shows.

39
00:02:44,374 --> 00:02:49,686
The destination is a tenant of this same installation, a separate site, so

40
00:02:49,686 --> 00:02:53,204
the address is local and the prefix is staging.

41
00:02:53,204 --> 00:02:56,324
A real destination uses an HTTPS address.

42
00:02:56,786 --> 00:03:00,699
The Client Name and the Api Key are the ones we created on

43
00:03:00,699 --> 00:03:03,816
the destination, and they must match exactly.

44
00:03:05,654 --> 00:03:10,427
We select Create, and a notification confirms the remote instance.

45
00:03:10,427 --> 00:03:16,194
The list shows Staging, with its Url under the name, and never shows the Api Key.

46
00:03:20,477 --> 00:03:26,478
Under Tools, Deployments, Plans, we select Manage Steps for a plan called Publish Pages.

47
00:03:26,478 --> 00:03:31,712
Its one step, Content, exports the published content items of the type Page.

48
00:03:31,712 --> 00:03:34,777
A separate video shows how to build plans.

49
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:36,608
We select Execute.

50
00:03:36,608 --> 00:03:41,586
Available Targets lists File Download, which saves the package on your computer, and

51
00:03:41,586 --> 00:03:43,799
one card for each remote instance.

52
00:03:43,799 --> 00:03:47,450
The Staging card sends the deployment plan to that instance.

53
00:03:47,984 --> 00:03:51,419
The Select button of the Staging card starts the deployment.

54
00:03:51,419 --> 00:03:56,310
The source builds the package and posts it to the destination, which imports it at once.

55
00:03:56,310 --> 00:03:58,184
Nothing asks for confirmation.

56
00:03:58,664 --> 00:03:59,729
We select it.

57
00:03:59,729 --> 00:04:06,625
A notification reads Deployment executed successfully, which means that the destination answered with the status 200.

58
00:04:06,625 --> 00:04:10,494
Whether the import itself worked, you check on the destination.

59
00:04:14,750 --> 00:04:18,695
Let's go back to Staging Site and open Content, Content Items.

60
00:04:18,695 --> 00:04:23,220
The two content items from the plan are listed, each with the type Page.

61
00:04:23,685 --> 00:04:28,294
The destination answers 200 even if importing the content of the package fails,

62
00:04:28,294 --> 00:04:32,035
so a quick look at its content is how you confirm a deployment.

63
00:04:36,235 --> 00:04:41,699
A remote deployment changes the destination without asking for confirmation.

64
00:04:41,699 --> 00:04:46,830
Use HTTPS, because the client name and Api Key travel with the package.

65
00:04:46,830 --> 00:04:51,495
Use a different Api Key for each pair of source and destination.

66
00:04:52,245 --> 00:04:57,735
Change keys regularly, and after any suspected leak, by editing the remote client

67
00:04:57,735 --> 00:04:59,629
and the remote instance.

68
00:04:59,629 --> 00:05:03,415
Delete remote clients and instances you no longer use.

69
00:05:03,415 --> 00:05:09,095
Treat exported packages as sensitive, since they can hold content and configuration.

70
00:05:09,845 --> 00:05:15,680
The permissions Manage remote instances and Manage remote clients control the two lists.

71
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:20,831
The Plans list needs Manage deployment plans and Export Data, and executing a

72
00:05:20,831 --> 00:05:22,631
plan needs Export Data.

73
00:05:22,631 --> 00:05:25,735
Administrators have all of these by default.

74
00:05:26,485 --> 00:05:32,077
If the destination answers with an error, a notification shows its status code.

75
00:05:32,077 --> 00:05:37,209
Check the Url, including any tenant prefix, and that the client name and

76
00:05:37,209 --> 00:05:39,446
Api Key match on both sites.

77
00:05:39,446 --> 00:05:43,525
A reverse proxy in front of it must allow large uploads.

78
00:05:44,275 --> 00:05:48,569
If the source reports success but the content is missing, check that the

79
00:05:48,569 --> 00:05:51,982
destination has the features that the plan's steps need.

80
00:05:51,982 --> 00:05:54,735
The destination's log records import errors.

81
00:05:56,735 --> 00:06:01,332
Remote deployment needs a remote client on the destination, a remote instance on

82
00:06:01,332 --> 00:06:03,470
the source, and a plan to execute.

83
00:06:03,470 --> 00:06:05,715
Check the result on the destination.

84
00:06:05,715 --> 00:06:07,105
Thanks for watching.

